# EVERY TEXT FILE CHECKS OUT AS LF, ON EVERY MACHINE (#71).
#
# The index has always been LF (`git ls-files --eol` → `i/lf` on all 2,531
# text files). What varied was the WORKING COPY: `core.autocrlf=true` on a
# Windows machine smudges a fresh checkout to CRLF, and seven suites that read
# source files and assert multi-line substrings containing a bare `\n` went red
# on every fresh `git worktree` while staying green on the long-lived main tree
# (never re-smudged) and on the ubuntu gate (autocrlf off). The class had
# already been paid for once by the Atlas's freshness fingerprint. Setting the
# ending here instead of in each reader kills it for every tool at once.
#
# `text=auto` keeps git's own binary detection (the 256 `-text` files stay
# bytes); `eol=lf` fixes the checkout ending for everything it calls text.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The pre-push gate is executed by `sh`, and a shell refusing its own shebang
# is a guard that is silently absent — the shape invariant 7 exists for. The
# rule above already covers it; this line stays so the one file whose endings
# are load-bearing says so by name.
.githooks/** text eol=lf
